Rollback protection:

  Once an hour this witness writes (log name, size, fingerprint) for every
  log it has signed into markovianprotocol.com/log, whose states seven outside
  witnesses sign and which is stamped into Bitcoin. On startup it reads the
  newest of those back, proves it's in a state that meets that 4-of-7 rule,
  and for each log keeps whichever mark is higher, published or stored.
  Recovery only ever raises a mark, so restoring an older witness.db can't
  lower one. An empty database is refused rather than trusted from scratch.
  If a published fingerprint contradicts what's stored at the same size,
  that log is held at 503 until an operator sorts it out.

  Because the marks are hourly, recovering from them alone could reopen up to
  an hour of progress. So recovery also asks each log it can fetch for its
  own current state, and raises the mark to that only if the state checks out
  against the pinned key and the log proves the mark is part of it. That's
  safe in the same one direction: a mark is a floor, it's stored without our
  signature, and a log that overstates its own size here can only make this
  witness refuse that log until it serves the version it signed. Logs that
  only send to us can't be asked, so they keep the one-hour gap. A log
  already held for contradicting itself is never raised.

  State of that check: confirmed (re-checked hourly; last 2026-11-10T17:21:43Z)
  leaf 14022, published 2026-11-10T16:20:01Z, checkpoint size 14026; 31 origins at or above the mark

Who runs it: Colin Winter, Markovian Protocol (SigmaSynth LLC),
hello@markovianprotocol.com. It runs on a Hetzner Cloud virtual machine in
Finland (2 vCPU, 4 GB, Ubuntu 22.04), the same machine as the Markovian
Protocol log, and is paid for by its operator; there is no outside funding.
It has run since 19 July 2026; its state moved with it to this machine on
25 July. A second server checks the log it shares the machine with every
hour. While we do intend to keep it up and running for the foreseeable
future, it is run on a best-effort basis. This means it might break, be
unavailable, or even have its state rolled back.

Contact: hello@markovianprotocol.com
